<LessonLayout title="Workbook &mdash; Jesus Christ, God and Man" cic="CCC 422-483" scripture="">

<section class="content">

<h2>Student Workbook</h2>
<p class="big-question">For use after class or in a group. Print this page or work in your notebook.</p>

<h3>Remembering the Essentials</h3>

<ol>
<li>What does the name "Jesus" mean in Hebrew? Why is it significant that God Himself chose this name?</li>
<li>What did the Council of Nicaea (325) teach about Christ's nature? Why does it matter for our salvation?</li>
<li>What does the Council of Chalcedon (451) define about the union of the divine and the human in Christ? Explain the four negations.</li>
<li>Name at least four titles or names of Jesus found in CCC 436&ndash;455 and explain what each reveals.</li>
<li>What does the word "kenosis" mean? How does it relate to the Incarnation and the Cross?</li>
<li>What are the four reasons the Catechism (CCC 456&ndash;460) gives for why God became man?</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>For Reflection and Writing</h3>

<p>Answer each question honestly. There are no "right" answers to these personal questions.</p>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">1. Jesus asks you today: "But you, who do you say that I am?" Don't answer with what you learned as a child, or what the Creed says. Write who Jesus is for you at this moment in your life.</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Be honest. If your answer is "I'm not sure," write that. That's where faith begins: with the truth of your actual situation.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">2. Jesus experienced hunger, weariness, tears, fear, anguish, and the feeling of abandonment. How does it change your prayer and your way of living through suffering to know that God understands your pain from the inside, not just from above?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Think of something you're suffering right now. Write Jesus one sentence about that pain, knowing He went through the same thing.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">3. The kenosis tells us that God emptied Himself out of love: He did not cling to His privileges, He stripped Himself and took the form of a servant (Phil 2:6&ndash;7). In what concrete areas of your life do you need to "empty" your ego, your pride, your need for control, or your desire for recognition?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Be concrete. Don't write generalities. Name a real situation where you can practice kenosis this week.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">4. Jesus spent thirty years in Nazareth working, obeying His parents, and living a completely normal life. Most of His earthly life was ordinary and hidden. What does that tell you about the value of your "normal" life and your daily work?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">How might you live your weekly routine &mdash; work, study, family &mdash; differently knowing that God chose to live like that for thirty years?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">5. C.S. Lewis posed the famous trilemma: Jesus, who claimed to be God, can only be mad, a liar, or the Lord. If you truly believe that Jesus is God made man, what concrete consequences does that have for your daily decisions? Does Jesus occupy the center of your life or only a "religious" corner on Sundays?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Don't answer what "is expected." Answer what you actually live and believe.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content" id="activities">

<h3>Practical Activities</h3>

<h4>A. Lectio Divina with John 1:1&ndash;14</h4>
<p>Read John's prologue three times, slowly. First, identify the word or phrase that stands out most. Second, ask: what is God saying to me through this word? Third, respond to God with a brief prayer from the heart. Write here the word that most struck you and what you understood.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

<h4>B. Contemplation of the Crucifix</h4>
<p>Take a crucifix or an image of Christ on the cross. For five minutes in silence, contemplate every detail: the pierced hands, the crown of thorns, the wound in His side, His face. Imagine that the man hanging there is God Himself, doing this freely out of love for you. Then write: "Lord, in your wounds I see..." and complete with what you've discovered.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

<h4>C. Letter to Jesus, God and Man</h4>
<p>Write a letter to Jesus beginning with: "Lord, I need you to be God and man because..." Tell Him what it means for you that He understands your humanity from the inside and that He has the divine power to save you. Tell Him something you've never told Him before.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

<h4>D. The Thirty Years of Nazareth</h4>
<p>List your typical weekday activities (waking up, breakfast, work/study, meals, chores, rest). Next to each one, write: "God chose to live this for thirty years." Then answer: how does this change how I see my day?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

<h4>E. Your Name and Jesus' Name</h4>
<p>Research the meaning of your own first name (online or by asking your parents). Write it here. Now compare: Jesus' name means "God saves." What does your name "say" about who you are? And more importantly: your name is not a coincidence to God. Write a brief prayer thanking God for calling you by your name (cf. Isaiah 43:1).</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

<h4>F. Mary's Fiat</h4>
<p>Read Lk 1:26&ndash;38. Mary said "yes" without knowing all the consequences. God is asking you to say "yes" to something today, even though you can't see the whole path. What is God inviting you to in this moment of your life that requires an act of trust like Mary's? Write your own "let it be done to me."</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Memory Verse</h3>

<div class="bible-passage">
<p>"And the Word became flesh and dwelt among us, and we have seen his glory, glory as of the only Son from the Father, full of grace and truth."</p>
<p class="bible-passage__ref">&mdash; John 1:14</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Prayer for This Week</h3>

<blockquote>
<p>Lord Jesus,<br />
You who, being God, became man,<br />
who emptied Your glory out of love for us,<br />
and walked our dusty roads,<br />
help me to know You<br />
not only with my mind but with my heart.<br />
May I not grow accustomed to the miracle of Your Incarnation,<br />
may I not read "and the Word became flesh"<br />
like old news.<br />
Give me John's wonder,<br />
Peter's faith,<br />
Mary's yes.<br />
And may my life, like Yours in Nazareth,<br />
hide an extraordinary love within the ordinary.<br />
Amen.</p>
</blockquote>

</section>

